WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.500
One register, kept by
the Civil Registration Office.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:08.900
Staff, other government systems, and now
an AI assistant that helps one person.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.600
Every request passes the office's rules.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.700
Each one gets only what the rules allow.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.600
The assistant can do less
than the person it helps.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.300
Alex asks to update a contact address.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.100
The assistant reads only
what the office allows.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.000
It prepares a draft and a review link.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.500
The draft stops there.
Only Alex can submit.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.600
Alex signs in on the official website.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.400
Compares, confirms, and submits.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.500
The request follows the office's own steps,
and the record changes.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.200
Every assistant action is recorded.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.400
Under pseudonyms,
with no names and no addresses.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.500
Assistants connect through
an open standard called MCP.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.800
Change one, or remove it.
The services keep working.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.900
Registry Stack contains no AI model.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.600
Now the office also wants
phone number corrections.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.800
An AI tool drafts
the change and the tests.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.500
The checks pass.
Refused cases stay refused.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.800
A person approves it.
Version five is published.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.000
AI can use your register.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.800
Your rules still apply.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.600
Registry Stack.
Open source, at registrystack.org.
